it runs a list of every tile that’s hit the table, who passed on what, who
hesitated, whose hand went toward one end of the line and changed its
mind. My brain keeps that list whether I ask it to or not. It’s the same
wiring that hears four conversations at once on the 4 train. Tonight it
was paying rent.
    By the third hand I knew the two men across from us were out of
fives. By the fourth I knew which one was sitting on the double six and
didn’t want to let go of it. I played into him until he had to. Two turns
later Tavito slapped his last tile down so hard the beer bottles jumped.
    “¡Capicúa! ¡Este muchacho cuenta!”
    This kid counts.
    I didn’t love the word. I smiled anyway, because he was delighted,
and because I was being paid in a drink I kept handing back.
    The men across the table weren’t delighted. The bigger one said
we’d been signaling. Tavito said the bigger one had been signaling
since 1983 and nobody had complained. The bigger one stood up, and
his chair went over. Cristian came out from behind the bars with a
broom held sideways, less a weapon than a statement, and said, “¡Ya!
¡Ya, ya, ya!” and everybody sat back down, breathing hard, over a
game of dominoes, at nine o’clock on a Monday.
    Tavito shook his head and turned his tiles face down.
    “Antes no era así,” he said to me, quietly. “We used to play here till
two in the morning, laughing. Now it’s a fight every week. Over a
doble cinco. Over nothing.” He shrugged. “Será el calor.”
    It must be the heat.
    The radio over the door was playing a merengue I knew from my
mother’s kitchen. I was tired, and full, and I had just won a hand of
dominoes in my mother’s country. I remembered the Friday fight out‐
side the bodega on Jerome over a lottery ticket. I filed it under jet lag
and picked up the bread.
    When I crossed back, Abuela was standing in the green doorway
with her arms folded.
    “¿Cuánto?”
    “Treinta.”
    “Bien.” She took the malta. “¿Jugaste con Tavito?”



